using System.Linq;
LINQ : Language Integrated Query :

LINQ Query Syntaxes
=> Query Syntax ( Declarative/SQL-like )
	SYNTAX : from <item> in <tablename> where <condition> select <item>;
	( starts with from, ends with select/group )
=> Method Syntax ( Fluent API : Method Chaining/Lambda Based )
	SYNTAX : <tablename>.Where(<condition>).Select(<item>=><item>);
	( allows method chaining, allows operations like Sum(), Count(), FirstOrDefault() and Aggregate())

QUERY EVALUATION : Defered vs Eager : When and how query is executed
=> Deferred Evaluation ( Lazy Execution )
		 : Query not executed immediately when defined
		 : Execution happens only when query is iterated : foreach, .ToList(), .Count(), .First();
		 : Query logic compiled but not executed until needed.
		 : When query results might change before execution.

=> Eager Evaluation ( Immediate Execution )
		 : Query executed immediately
		 : Results stored in memory right away
		 : Terminal method - .ToList(), .ToArray(), .Count(), .First(), .ToDictionary()

QUERY EXECUTION : Interpreted Query vs Local Query
=> Interpreted Queries ( IQueryable<T> )
		: Queries executed on Remote/DataBase
		: IQueryable<T> : represents queryable datasource
		: Query deferred and translated into sql query for execution
		: Works with - Expression Trees : converts C# Linq query into sql query
		: Query logic executed on database server
		: Ensures only required data is fetched
		: LINQ -> ExpressionTree -> SQL Query -> executed on DB Server

=> Local Queries ( IEnumerable<T> )
		: Queries executed on In-Memory/Collections
		: Execution happens in C# runtime 
		: implements deferred execution, but filtering happens after data loaded
		: uses Iterator to loopm through data in memory
		: iterates over each item in the collection and applies filtering logic.
		: all data loaded in memory first then Linq filters it
		: No SQL generated.

EXPRESSION TREE of a LINQ query
=> What : Represents a query as an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST)
		: allows LINQ providers to analyze and convert queries dynamically before execution
		: LINQ Query with IQueryable<T> allows coversion

Anatomy of an Expression : Each expression node in the tree belongs to a specific Expression Type from the System.Linq.Expressions namespace.
=> Parametr Expression
		: named parameter used in a lambda expression
		: forms input variable for an expression tree
=> Member Expression
		: Represents access to field/property of an object/class
		: where(u=>u.Age>18)
=> Method Call Expression
		: Represents a method call inside an expression tree.
		:.OrderBy(u => u.Name.ToLower())
=> Binary Expression
		: Represents binary operations like +, -, *, /, ==, &&, etc
=> Unary Expression
		: Represents an operation on a single operand, such as negation (-), type conversion ((int)x), or logical NOT (!x).

# Projection Operators ---- 
" Projection is the process of transforming data from a collection (or sequence) into a new form"
=> SELECT - IEnumerable<TResult> Select(Func<TSource, TResult> selector); 
		: TSource → Type of elements in the source collection. : The source is an IEnumerable<T>, meaning it's an iterable sequence
		: TResult → Type of elements in the resulting collection.		
		: Transforms each element individually, Returns one output per input element.
✅ Extracting specific columns (fields or properties) from objects.
✅ Transforming data into a different type.
✅ Converting numerical operations (e.g., square, log, etc.).
(1-to-1 mapping): Collection.Select(emp => emp.Name) → ["Alice", "Bob"]	

=> SELECTMANY - IEnumerable<TResult> SelectMany(Func<TSource, IEnumerable<TResult>> selector);
		: The source collection is iterated. The selector function is applied to get a nested collection 
		: A nested loop iterates through the inner collection, extracting and returning elements one at a time.
		: Flattens and transforms collections inside a collection.
		: Flattens nested collections and then applies a transformation.
		: Used when each element contains a collection, and we want to work with the inner elements directly.
		: Returns a single flattened sequence instead of nested collections.
		: Returns a different number of outputs per input (can be more than one per input).
✅ Flattening nested lists, arrays, or objects containing lists.
✅ Database queries where relationships involve one-to-many (e.g., Orders per Customer).
(1-to-many mapping): Collection.SelectMany(emp => emp.Orders) → ["Laptop", "Phone", "Tablet"]

# Restriction/Filtering Operators ----
=> WHERE - IEnumerable<TSource> Where(Func<TSource, bool> predicate);
		: The Where operator filters elements based on a predicate (condition).
		: It only includes elements that match the condition. Only yield elements that satisfy the condition

# Ordering/Sorting Operators ----
=> ORDERBY ( Sort by a primary key )
		: OrderBy sorts elements in ascending order.
		: If sorting numbers, it sorts from smallest to largest.
		: If sorting strings, it sorts alphabetically (A → Z).
		: If sorting objects, it sorts based on the provided property.
		: Internally uses QuickSort, MergeSort, or HeapSort (depending on implementation).
		: Calls IComparer<T>.Compare() or uses default comparer (IComparable<T>).
		: Uses lazy evaluation, meaning sorting does not happen immediately.
✅ If sorting objects, ensure the property being sorted has a proper comparison method (e.g., IComparable).
✅ Stable sorting: Elements with equal keys maintain their original relative order.

=> THENBY
		: ThenBy is used after OrderBy or OrderByDescending.
		: It performs secondary sorting in ascending order.
		: If two elements have the same primary key, ThenBy sorts by secondary key.
		: Uses stable sorting, meaning equal elements maintain their relative order.
		: Instead of performing another full sort, it sorts only within groups of identical primary keys.
		: Uses linked lists or additional keys to track secondary sorting.

=> ORDERBYDESCENDING 
		: OrderByDescending sorts in reverse order.
=> THENBYDESCENDING
		: ThenByDescending sorts the secondary key in descending order.

# Grouping Operators ----
=> GROUPBY
		: used to group elements in a collection based on a specific key.
		: To create nested collections (each group becomes a collection of items).
		: To structure hierarchical data (grouping orders by customer, employees by department, etc.).
		: var groupedResult = collection.GroupBy(item => item.KeySelector);
		: The result is an IEnumerable<IGrouping<TKey, TElement>>, where:
			TKey = Grouping Key
			TElement = Items in the group
		: Grouping Key Cannot be Null: Ensure the key used for grouping is not null, or use .Where() to filter out null values.
		: GroupBy uses deferred execution, meaning it doesn’t execute until iterated.
		: By default, GroupBy does not maintain order; use .OrderBy() if needed.
		: Supports Multiple Key Grouping: You can group by multiple keys using anonymous objects
			var grouped = products.GroupBy(p => new { p.Category, p.Price });
		: GroupBy alone returns IGrouping<TKey, TElement>, use .Select() to project results.
		: Hash Table Implementation (For small data) Each key is stored as a hash.
GroupBy -> Deferred Execution + Throws Exception if Key Missing
ToLookup -> Immediate Execution + Returns Empty Collection if Key Missing

# Join Operations ----
Used to combine data from multiple collections or tables based on a common key.
=> JOIN
		: Performs an Inner Join.
		: Used to fetch matching records from two datasets based on a common key.

var result = collection1.Join(collection2,
                keyFromCollection1 => keyFromCollection1.Property,  // Outer key
                keyFromCollection2 => keyFromCollection2.Property,  // Inner key
                (outerItem, innerItem) => new { outerItem, innerItem } // Result Selector
);

=> GROUPJOIN
		: Performs a Left Join with Grouping.
		: Used for Left Join, but instead of getting NULL for missing data, it groups the matching items.

var result = collection1.GroupJoin(collection2,
                keyFromCollection1 => keyFromCollection1.Property,  // Outer key
                keyFromCollection2 => keyFromCollection2.Property,  // Inner key
                (outerItem, innerItems) => new { outerItem, innerItems } // Result Selector
);
		: 

=> DEFAULTIFEMPTY
		: Helps in Left Outer Joins (for NULL handling).
		: Used with GroupJoin to simulate a true LEFT JOIN by returning NULL where no match exists.

Need only matching records?						: Use Inner Join (Join)
Need all left-table records even if no match?	: Use Left Join (GroupJoin + DefaultIfEmpty)
Need all right-table records even if no match?	: Use Right Join (Simulated Left Join)
Need all records from both tables?				: Use Full Outer Join (Union)
Need every possible combination?				: Use Cross Join (from...from)
Need hierarchical relationships?				: Use Self Join (Join on itself)

# Partition Operators ----
=> TAKE 
		: Used to retrieve the first N elements from a sequence.
		: var result = collection.Take(n);

=> TAKEWHILE
		: Retrieves elements as long as a condition is true.
		: var result = collection.TakeWhile(condition);

=> SKIP
		: Skips the first N elements and returns the rest.
		: var result = collection.Skip(n);

=> SKIPWHILE
		: Skips elements while a condition is true.
		: var result = collection.SkipWhile(condition);

# Aggregator Operators ----
=> COUNT	 : var count = collection.Count();
=> SUM		 : var sum = collection.Sum(x => x.Property);
=> MIN		 : var min = collection.Min();
=> MAX		 : var max = collection.Max();
=> AVERAGE	 : var avg = collection.Average();
=> AGGREGATE : var result = collection.Aggregate((x, y) => x + y); : Applies a function over elements.

# Set Operators ----
=> DISTINCT  : var distinctItems = collection.Distinct();
=> UNION	 : var result = collection1.Union(collection2);
=> INTERSECT : var result = collection1.Intersect(collection2);
=> EXCEPT	 : var result = collection1.Except(collection2);

# Conversion Operators ----
=> TOARRAY()		: var array = collection.ToArray();
=> TOLIST()			: var list = collection.ToList();
=> TODICTIONARY()	: var dict = collection.ToDictionary(x => x.Key);
=> OFTYPE			: var integers = collection.OfType<int>(); : Filters elements by type.

# Element Operators ----
=> FIRST()			: var first = collection.First();
=> FIRSTORDEFAULT() : var firstOrDefault = collection.FirstOrDefault();
=> LAST()			: var last = collection.Last();
=> LASTORDEFAULT()	: var lastOrDefault = collection.LastOrDefault();
=> SINGLE			: var single = collection.Single(); : Ensures only one element exists, throws exception if multiple.
=> SINGLEORDEFAULT	: var singleOrDefault = collection.SingleOrDefault();
=> ELEMENTAT		: var item = collection.ElementAt(2);

# Generate Sequences ----
=> ENUMERABLE.RANGE  : ✔️ Generates range of numbers.
					 : var range = Enumerable.Range(1, 5); // {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
=> ENUMERABLE.REPEAT : ✔️ Generates repeated values
					 : var repeated = Enumerable.Repeat("Hello", 3);

# Sequence Operators ----
=> SEQUENCEEQUAL	 : var isEqual = collection1.SequenceEqual(collection2);
=> CONCAT			 : var result = collection1.Concat(collection2);
=> ZIP				 : var result = collection1.Zip(collection2, (x, y) => x + " " + y); ✔️ Combines two collections.

# Quantifying Members ----
: These operators are used to evaluate conditions across a collection.
=> ANY : ✔️ Checks if any element in the collection satisfies a condition.
	   : bool result = collection.Any(condition);

=> ALL : ✔️ Checks if all elements in the collection satisfy a condition.
	   : bool result = collection.All(condition);

# Query Expression ----
=> LET	: ✔️ Creates a temporary variable inside a LINQ query.
		: var result = from x in collection  
             let tempVar = someExpression  
             where condition  
             select tempVar;

=> INTO : ✔️ Allows chaining queries and continues LINQ expressions.
		: var result = from x in collection  
             group x by x.Property into newGroup  
             select newGroup;

# Parallel LINQ (PLINQ)
Operations:
		: .AsParallel() => Enables parallel execution.
		: .WithDegreeOfParallelism(n)=> Limits parallel threads.
		: .ForAll() => Executes results in parallel.
		: .AsSequential() => Reverts back to sequential execution.

# SELECT() and SELECTMANY()
	string[] words = { "Hello", "World", "LINQ" };
	var firstLetters = words.Select(w => w[0]);
	var characters = words.SelectMany(w => w.ToCharArray());
	Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", characters));   // Output: H, e, l, l, o, W, o, r, l, d, L, I, N, Q
	Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", firstLetters)); // Output: H, W, L

	var students = new List<Student>
	{
		new Student { Name = "Alice", Subjects = new List<string> { "Math", "Physics" } },
		new Student { Name = "Bob", Subjects = new List<string> { "Chemistry" } }
	};
	var studentNames = students.Select(s => s.Name);
	var allSubjects = students.SelectMany(s => s.Subjects);
	Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", allSubjects));// Output: Math, Physics, Chemistry
	Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", studentNames));// Output: Alice, Bob

# WHERE()
	var results = numbers.Where(n => n % 2 == 0 && n > 3);
	Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", results)); // Output: 4, 6
	
	var indexedResults = numbers.Where((n, index) => index % 2 == 0);
	Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", indexedResults)); // Output: 1, 3, 5 (Even indexed elements)

# ORDERBY(), ORDERBYDESCENDING(), THENBY(), THENBYDESCENDING()
	var employees = new List<Employee>
	{
		new Employee { Name = "Alice", Department = "HR", Salary = 5000 },
		new Employee { Name = "Bob", Department = "IT", Salary = 7000 },
		new Employee { Name = "Charlie", Department = "IT", Salary = 6000 },
		new Employee { Name = "David", Department = "HR", Salary = 4500 }
	};

	var sortedEmployees = employees.OrderBy(e => e.Department)
								   .ThenByDescending(e => e.Salary);

	foreach (var emp in sortedEmployees)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{emp.Name} - {emp.Department} - {emp.Salary}");
	}
	// Output:
	// David - HR - 4500
	// Alice - HR - 5000
	// Bob - IT - 7000
	// Charlie - IT - 6000

# GROUPBY()
	var departmentCounts = employees.GroupBy(e => e.Department)
                                .Select(group => new 
                                { 
                                    Department = group.Key, 
                                    Count = group.Count() 
                                });

	foreach (var dept in departmentCounts)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{dept.Department}: {dept.Count} employees");
	}
	class Product
	{
		public string Category { get; set; }
		public string Name { get; set; }
		public double Price { get; set; }
	}
	var products = new List<Product>
	{
		new Product { Category = "Electronics", Name = "Laptop", Price = 800 },
		new Product { Category = "Electronics", Name = "Phone", Price = 500 },
		new Product { Category = "Clothing", Name = "Shirt", Price = 30 },
		new Product { Category = "Clothing", Name = "Jeans", Price = 50 }
	};
	var groupedProducts = products.GroupBy(p => p.Category)
								  .Select(group => new 
								  { 
									  Category = group.Key, 
									  TotalPrice = group.Sum(p => p.Price) 
								  });

	foreach (var category in groupedProducts)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{category.Category}: Total Price = {category.TotalPrice}");
	}
	#Tolookup
	var lookup = employees.ToLookup(e => e.Department);
	foreach (var group in lookup)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{group.Key}: {group.Count()} employees");
	}

# JOIN (INNER JOIN) : An Inner Join returns only matching records from both collections.
	var employees = new List<Employee>
	{
		new Employee { Id = 1, Name = "Alice", DepartmentId = 1 },
		new Employee { Id = 2, Name = "Bob", DepartmentId = 2 },
		new Employee { Id = 3, Name = "Charlie", DepartmentId = 3 }
	};

	var departments = new List<Department>
	{
		new Department { Id = 1, Name = "HR" },
		new Department { Id = 2, Name = "IT" }
	};

	var result = employees.Join(departments,
								e => e.DepartmentId,  // Employee.DepartmentId
								d => d.Id,            // Department.Id
								(e, d) => new { e.Name, DepartmentName = d.Name });

	foreach (var item in result)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{item.Name} works in {item.DepartmentName}");
	}
	Alice works in HR
	Bob works in IT
	Charlie is excluded because there’s no matching DepartmentId.

# GROUPJOIN (LEFT JOIN)
	var result = departments.GroupJoin(employees,
                                   d => d.Id,
                                   e => e.DepartmentId,
                                   (d, empGroup) => new { Department = d.Name, Employees = empGroup });

	foreach (var group in result)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{group.Department}: {string.Join(", ", group.Employees.Select(e => e.Name))}");
	}
	HR: Alice
	IT: Bob
	Finance:   // No employees in Finance

# GROUPJOIN + DEFAULTIFEMPTY() : A Left Join returns all records from the left collection and matched records from the right (or NULL if no match).

	var result = departments.GroupJoin(employees,
                                   d => d.Id,
                                   e => e.DepartmentId,
                                   (d, empGroup) => new { Department = d.Name, Employees = empGroup.DefaultIfEmpty() });

	foreach (var group in result)
	{
		foreach (var emp in group.Employees)
		{
			Console.WriteLine($"{group.Department}: {(emp != null ? emp.Name : "No Employee")}");
		}
	}
	HR: Alice
	IT: Bob
	Finance: No Employee
	Now "Finance" shows explicitly that it has "No Employee" instead of an empty result.

# RIGHTJOIN (Simulated using Left Join) : A Right Join returns all records from the right collection and matched records from the left.
	LINQ does not have a direct Right Join, but we can swap the table order and use Left Join.
	var result = departments.GroupJoin(employees,
                                   d => d.Id,
                                   e => e.DepartmentId,
                                   (d, empGroup) => new { Department = d, Employees = empGroup.DefaultIfEmpty() });

	foreach (var group in result)
	{
		foreach (var emp in group.Employees)
		{
			Console.WriteLine($"{group.Department.Name} - {(emp != null ? emp.Name : "No Employee")}");
		}
	}
	HR - Alice
	IT - Bob
	Finance - No Employee

# FULLOUTERJOIN : A Full Outer Join returns all records from both collections, inserting NULL when no match is found.
	LINQ does not support Full Outer Join directly, but we can achieve it using Union + Left Join + Right Join.
	var leftJoin = employees.GroupJoin(departments,
                                   e => e.DepartmentId,
                                   d => d.Id,
                                   (e, deptGroup) => new { Employee = e.Name, Department = deptGroup.Select(d => d.Name).FirstOrDefault() });

	var rightJoin = departments.GroupJoin(employees,
										  d => d.Id,
										  e => e.DepartmentId,
										  (d, empGroup) => new { Employee = empGroup.Select(e => e.Name).FirstOrDefault(), Department = d.Name });

	var fullOuterJoin = leftJoin.Union(rightJoin);

	foreach (var item in fullOuterJoin)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{item.Employee ?? "No Employee"} - {item.Department ?? "No Department"}");
	}
	Alice - HR
	Bob - IT
	Charlie - No Department
	No Employee - Finance

# CROSSJOIN : A Cross Join creates a Cartesian Product (every combination of records from both tables).
	var result = from e in employees
             from d in departments
             select new { e.Name, Department = d.Name };

	foreach (var item in result)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{item.Name} - {item.Department}");
	}
	Alice - HR
	Alice - IT
	Alice - Finance
	Bob - HR
	Bob - IT
	Bob - Finance
	Charlie - HR
	Charlie - IT
	Charlie - Finance

# SELFJOIN : A Self Join is used to join a table to itself, often for hierarchical relationships like managers and employees.
	var result = employees.Join(employees,
                            manager => manager.Id,
                            employee => employee.ManagerId,
                            (manager, employee) => new { Employee = employee.Name, Manager = manager.Name });

	foreach (var item in result)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{item.Employee} reports to {item.Manager}");
	}
	Alice reports to Bob
	Charlie reports to Alice

